\subtree{
  \title{AI vegetarian}
  \p{I've been thinking for a while about publishing an official statement regarding my stance on LLMs (and other generative AI systems), but it always felt too daunting to express what I wanted to express.  But I recently found several things other people had written that captured my position pretty well, so I [finally published a statement](009L).  I still want to write something for my students in particular, but I'm still thinking about that.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Blog post series on constructive logic}

  \p{Following last week's discussion on [[00BM]], I realized that there is something of a gap in the pedagogical materials available on constructive logic, and the difference between classical and constructive logic. There are many hand-wavy, intuitive explanations of constructive vs non-constructive proofs in, say, slide decks used for teaching undergraduate Discrete Math courses.  There is also lots of advanced material, such as Andrej Bauer's paper [Five Stages of Accepting Constructive Mathematics](https://math.andrej.com/2016/10/10/five-stages-of-accepting-constructive-mathematics/), the [HoTT book](https://homotopytypetheory.org/book/), and others.  However, there does not seem to be much intermediate-level material.  So I plan to write a series of blog posts aimed at people who are familiar with typed functional programming, and perhaps have even heard of the Curry-Howard correspondence, and perhaps have even played with a theorem prover such as Agda or Lean, but who don't know the actual formal details of classical vs constructive logic.}

  \p{Earlier this week I started drafting some ideas on paper; I hope this week or next to find some time to start writing it up more formally.}
}

